1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,070
It is the morning of July twenty fifth, two thousand.

2
00:00:04,070 --> 00:00:10,050
At an airport outside Paris, a hundred holidaymakers are boarding the most beautiful aircraft ever built.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:19,402
It is a machine that flies faster than a rifle bullet, on the edge of space, in a shape so perfect that grown engineers still call it a work of art.

4
00:00:20,533 --> 00:00:25,924
In the cockpit sits Captain Christian Marty, a veteran pilot who once windsurfed across the Atlantic.

5
00:00:25,924 --> 00:00:30,642
In ninety seconds, he will be fighting for the lives of everyone on board.

6
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:37,914
Because lying on the runway ahead, invisible to the crew, is a thin strip of metal no longer than a ruler.

7
00:00:37,914 --> 00:00:41,719
And it is about to bring down the fastest airliner in the world.

8
00:00:42,866 --> 00:00:45,301
One hundred and thirteen people will die.

9
00:00:45,301 --> 00:00:49,475
And within three years, every Concorde on Earth will be grounded forever.

10
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:54,934
This is the story of a machine two nations built to touch the future.

11
00:00:54,934 --> 00:00:58,030
How it became the most glamorous object in the sky.

12
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:01,127
And how a single, freak accident ended the supersonic age.

13
00:01:02,266 --> 00:01:03,755
You're watching mindwired.

14
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:06,781
The rise and fall of Concorde.

15
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:11,688
Chapter one. Two nations, one impossible idea.

16
00:01:12,833 --> 00:01:19,599
To understand Concorde, you have to go back to a time when the future felt like something you could build.

17
00:01:19,599 --> 00:01:23,321
The nineteen fifties. The jet age. And a simple, intoxicating idea.

18
00:01:23,321 --> 00:01:26,704
What if a passenger plane could fly faster than sound?

19
00:01:27,833 --> 00:01:31,188
It was not a crazy idea. Fighter jets already flew supersonic.

20
00:01:31,188 --> 00:01:33,322
The dream was to shrink the world.

21
00:01:33,322 --> 00:01:40,032
To cross the Atlantic in the time it takes to watch a film, and land before you took off, by the clock.

22
00:01:41,166 --> 00:01:44,925
But building it was almost impossibly hard, and almost impossibly expensive.

23
00:01:44,925 --> 00:01:49,368
So hard that no single country could afford to fail at it alone.

24
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:54,294
Britain and France had each been quietly working on the same dream.

25
00:01:54,294 --> 00:01:57,139
And in nineteen sixty two, they did something extraordinary.

26
00:01:57,139 --> 00:01:59,668
They signed a treaty to build it together.

27
00:02:00,799 --> 00:02:04,118
And that word matters. It was not a business contract.

28
00:02:04,118 --> 00:02:10,424
It was a binding treaty between two governments, written so that pulling out would cost a fortune in penalties.

29
00:02:10,424 --> 00:02:15,401
That single clause is the reason Concorde was never cancelled, even when everyone wanted to.

30
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:21,444
The British firm was the British Aircraft Corporation. The French, Sud Aviation.

31
00:02:21,444 --> 00:02:28,810
Between them, hundreds of companies, tens of thousands of workers, two languages, two systems of measurement, one machine.

32
00:02:29,933 --> 00:02:32,302
Even the name was a negotiation.

33
00:02:32,302 --> 00:02:36,250
The French president, Charles de Gaulle, chose Concorde, meaning harmony.

34
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:40,199
Britain, prickly, dropped the French e and spelled it Concord.

35
00:02:41,333 --> 00:02:48,445
For years it was a diplomatic sore point, until a British minister named Tony Benn quietly gave the e back.

36
00:02:48,445 --> 00:02:52,001
It stood, he said, for excellence, England, Europe, and entente.

37
00:02:52,001 --> 00:02:56,980
When a Scot complained, he added that the e was also for Écosse. Scotland.

38
00:02:58,099 --> 00:03:00,385
But Britain and France were not alone.

39
00:03:00,385 --> 00:03:04,957
This was the Cold War, and supersonic flight was a matter of national pride.

40
00:03:04,957 --> 00:03:07,895
Two other giants were racing for the same prize.

41
00:03:09,033 --> 00:03:13,905
In America, Boeing was designing a monster. The two seven oh seven.

42
00:03:13,905 --> 00:03:20,808
Bigger, faster, meant to carry nearly three hundred people at almost three times the speed of sound.

43
00:03:20,808 --> 00:03:23,244
On paper, it would crush Concorde.

44
00:03:24,366 --> 00:03:29,126
But the American plane grew too heavy, too complex, and too expensive.

45
00:03:29,126 --> 00:03:35,076
In nineteen seventy one, before a single prototype ever flew, Congress cut off its funding.

46
00:03:35,076 --> 00:03:39,439
The American dream of supersonic travel was dead in the workshop.

47
00:03:40,566 --> 00:03:45,564
The other rival was the Soviet Union. And they did something Concorde could not.

48
00:03:45,564 --> 00:03:46,992
They got there first.

49
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:53,517
The Tupolev Tu one forty four looked so much like Concorde that the West nicknamed it Concordski.

50
00:03:53,517 --> 00:03:57,951
It flew two months before Concorde, at the very end of nineteen sixty eight.

51
00:03:57,951 --> 00:04:00,802
For a moment, the Soviets had won the race.

52
00:04:01,933 --> 00:04:10,858
But in June nineteen seventy three, in front of a huge crowd at the Paris Air Show, a Tu one forty four broke apart in the sky and fell onto a village below.

53
00:04:10,858 --> 00:04:13,562
Six crew and eight people on the ground were killed.

54
00:04:13,562 --> 00:04:15,726
To this day, no one knows exactly why.

55
00:04:16,866 --> 00:04:21,637
The Soviet supersonic dream never really recovered. Which left just one machine standing.

56
00:04:21,637 --> 00:04:24,572
The one Britain and France had built together.

57
00:04:25,699 --> 00:04:27,501
Chapter two. The machine.

58
00:04:28,633 --> 00:04:34,829
On the second of March, nineteen sixty nine, in Toulouse, a slim white prototype rolled onto the runway.

59
00:04:34,829 --> 00:04:38,272
At the controls was the French test pilot André Turcat.

60
00:04:39,399 --> 00:04:43,010
For twenty eight minutes, Concorde flew for the first time.

61
00:04:43,010 --> 00:04:48,064
A month later, the British prototype took off from Filton, flown by Brian Trubshaw.

62
00:04:48,064 --> 00:04:49,508
The dream was airborne.

63
00:04:50,633 --> 00:04:55,689
By late nineteen seventy, it had done what it was built to do. Mach two.

64
00:04:55,689 --> 00:04:57,374
Twice the speed of sound.

65
00:04:57,374 --> 00:05:01,082
And everything about how it got there was strange, and brilliant.

66
00:05:02,199 --> 00:05:03,367
Start with the wing.

67
00:05:03,367 --> 00:05:08,327
Not the long, straight wing of a normal jet, but a great sweeping triangle, an ogival delta.

68
00:05:08,327 --> 00:05:14,163
It let Concorde slice through the air at twice the speed of sound, and still float gently down to land.

69
00:05:15,299 --> 00:05:16,402
Then the engines.

70
00:05:16,402 --> 00:05:24,123
Four Rolls-Royce Olympus turbojets, with afterburners, called reheat, that dumped raw fuel into the exhaust for a brutal kick of thrust.

71
00:05:24,123 --> 00:05:30,372
Concorde is the only passenger plane ever to fly for decades with afterburners, like a fighter jet.

72
00:05:31,499 --> 00:05:36,254
And the nose. To fly fast, Concorde needed a long, sharp, pointed nose.

73
00:05:36,254 --> 00:05:41,740
But that nose blocked the pilots' view on landing. So the engineers made it move.

74
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:47,225
The famous droop nose, that tilted down for takeoff and landing, then raised for flight.

75
00:05:48,366 --> 00:05:50,293
It cruised at sixty thousand feet.

76
00:05:50,293 --> 00:05:57,679
So high that passengers could see the curve of the Earth, and the sky above them turning a deep, dark blue toward black.

77
00:05:57,679 --> 00:06:01,532
You were, in every way that mattered, on the edge of space.

78
00:06:02,666 --> 00:06:07,693
It flew so fast that friction heated its skin to over one hundred degrees Celsius.

79
00:06:07,693 --> 00:06:14,395
The whole aircraft physically stretched, growing several inches longer in flight, then shrinking again as it cooled on the ground.

80
00:06:15,533 --> 00:06:21,395
But all that magic came at a price. The afterburners drank fuel. The routes were limited.

81
00:06:21,395 --> 00:06:29,090
And the whole programme cost a staggering amount, around one point three billion pounds, many times what anyone had first estimated.

82
00:06:30,233 --> 00:06:34,080
There was one more limit, and it would quietly shape everything.

83
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:41,074
When Concorde went supersonic, it dragged a sonic boom across the ground below, a thunderclap that rattled windows for miles.

84
00:06:42,199 --> 00:06:46,075
So country after country banned it from flying supersonic over land.

85
00:06:46,075 --> 00:06:51,360
And that single fact meant Concorde could only truly stretch its legs over the ocean.

86
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:56,645
Its future, it turned out, would live and die on one route. Across the Atlantic.

87
00:06:57,766 --> 00:06:59,960
Chapter three. The golden age.

88
00:07:01,099 --> 00:07:08,091
On the twenty first of January, nineteen seventy six, at exactly the same moment, two Concordes took off.

89
00:07:08,091 --> 00:07:10,421
One from London, one from Paris.

90
00:07:10,421 --> 00:07:15,858
After all the years and all the billions, Concorde was finally carrying paying passengers.

91
00:07:16,999 --> 00:07:20,685
Only two airlines ever flew it. British Airways, and Air France.

92
00:07:20,685 --> 00:07:24,705
The national carriers of the two nations that had dreamed it up.

93
00:07:25,833 --> 00:07:29,418
The prize was New York. But New York did not want it.

94
00:07:29,418 --> 00:07:32,106
The airport authority banned Concorde, terrified of the noise.

95
00:07:32,106 --> 00:07:37,484
It took a fight all the way to the United States Supreme Court to force the doors open.

96
00:07:38,633 --> 00:07:43,084
In nineteen seventy seven, Concorde won. And the route that defined it began.

97
00:07:43,084 --> 00:07:47,535
London and Paris, to New York, in around three and a half hours.

98
00:07:47,535 --> 00:07:51,302
Less than half the time of any other airliner on Earth.

99
00:07:52,433 --> 00:07:57,874
And Concorde became something no aircraft had ever been. Not just transport. A symbol.

100
00:07:57,874 --> 00:08:02,150
Of glamour, of money, of a future that had actually arrived.

101
00:08:03,299 --> 00:08:08,863
A ticket cost a fortune, well over ten thousand dollars round trip in its later years.

102
00:08:08,863 --> 00:08:13,035
The cabin was small and narrow, but the passengers did not care.

103
00:08:13,035 --> 00:08:18,946
Kings, presidents, film stars, rock stars, and bankers who simply had to be in Manhattan by lunchtime.

104
00:08:20,066 --> 00:08:27,361
They drank champagne at the edge of space and watched the sky turn black, while the little display at the front of the cabin ticked past Mach two.

105
00:08:27,361 --> 00:08:29,705
There has never been anything else quite like it.

106
00:08:30,833 --> 00:08:32,220
And it was fast.

107
00:08:32,220 --> 00:08:42,280
On the seventh of February, nineteen ninety six, a British Airways Concorde crossed the Atlantic, New York to London, in two hours, fifty two minutes, and fifty nine seconds.

108
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:45,749
A record for a passenger aircraft that still stands today.

109
00:08:46,866 --> 00:08:49,467
For twenty four years, this was the picture.

110
00:08:49,467 --> 00:08:55,320
The most beautiful machine in the sky, doing the impossible, every single day, as if it were nothing.

111
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:59,222
It felt permanent. It felt like the future was here to stay.

112
00:09:00,366 --> 00:09:04,365
But there was a flaw. Hidden in the wing. In the tyres.

113
00:09:04,365 --> 00:09:07,032
In physics no one had fully reckoned with.

114
00:09:07,032 --> 00:09:11,364
And on one ordinary summer afternoon, it would all come due at once.

115
00:09:12,499 --> 00:09:14,772
Chapter four. Ninety seconds.

116
00:09:15,899 --> 00:09:17,910
July twenty fifth, two thousand. Paris.

117
00:09:17,910 --> 00:09:22,935
A Concorde is being readied not for a scheduled airline flight, but for a charter.

118
00:09:22,935 --> 00:09:29,300
A hundred passengers, most of them German tourists, on their way to New York to board a luxury cruise.

119
00:09:30,433 --> 00:09:33,366
It is a happy flight. A holiday flight.

120
00:09:33,366 --> 00:09:40,699
Families, couples, retirees, all of them about to ride the fastest airliner in the world for the very first time.

121
00:09:41,833 --> 00:09:45,380
But minutes earlier, another aircraft had used the same runway.

122
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:48,927
A Continental Airlines DC ten, bound for the United States.

123
00:09:48,927 --> 00:09:57,794
And as it took off, according to investigators, a thin strip of titanium metal fell from one of its engines, and lay on the tarmac.

124
00:09:58,933 --> 00:10:01,548
Nobody saw it. Nobody swept it up.

125
00:10:01,548 --> 00:10:07,527
It was just there, waiting, as Concorde lined up and the engines spooled to full power.

126
00:10:08,666 --> 00:10:10,704
Concorde begins its take off roll.

127
00:10:10,704 --> 00:10:20,552
It accelerates down the runway, faster and faster, past the point of no return, the speed pilots call V one, where you are committed to fly no matter what.

128
00:10:21,699 --> 00:10:27,182
And then, at high speed, the left main tyre runs over the strip of metal. The tyre bursts.

129
00:10:27,182 --> 00:10:29,009
Not a slow leak, an explosion.

130
00:10:29,009 --> 00:10:35,100
A chunk of rubber weighing about four and a half kilograms is hurled up into the underside of the wing.

131
00:10:36,233 --> 00:10:41,175
Here is the part that still astonishes engineers. The rubber did not pierce the fuel tank.

132
00:10:41,175 --> 00:10:42,720
It did not have to.

133
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:48,589
The impact sent a violent shockwave through the fuel inside, and that pressure wave ruptured the tank from within.

134
00:10:49,733 --> 00:10:51,855
Fuel pours out in a sheet.

135
00:10:51,855 --> 00:10:57,513
And somewhere in the landing gear, investigators believe, an electric spark most likely set it alight.

136
00:10:57,513 --> 00:11:03,526
In an instant, a trail of fire is streaming from the wing, longer than the aircraft itself.

137
00:11:04,666 --> 00:11:08,148
In the cockpit, alarms. Two of the four engines lose power.

138
00:11:08,148 --> 00:11:11,313
But Concorde is already past V one. It cannot stop.

139
00:11:11,313 --> 00:11:15,428
It has no choice but to fly, on a wing that is burning.

140
00:11:16,566 --> 00:11:21,715
Captain Marty and his crew do everything they can. They coax it into the air.

141
00:11:21,715 --> 00:11:24,117
But the landing gear will not retract.

142
00:11:24,117 --> 00:11:28,579
The drag is too great, the thrust too little, the fire too fierce.

143
00:11:28,579 --> 00:11:31,325
The aircraft cannot climb, and it cannot accelerate.

144
00:11:32,466 --> 00:11:34,484
For about ninety seconds, they fight.

145
00:11:34,484 --> 00:11:43,228
And then, unable to stay in the air, Concorde rolls, and comes down onto a small hotel in the town of Gonesse, just outside the airport.

146
00:11:44,366 --> 00:11:47,631
All one hundred passengers and nine crew on board are killed.

147
00:11:47,631 --> 00:11:50,600
Four more people die on the ground, in the hotel.

148
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:54,162
One hundred and thirteen lives, gone, in a minute and a half.

149
00:11:55,299 --> 00:11:59,498
It was the first fatal crash in Concorde's twenty four year history.

150
00:11:59,498 --> 00:12:06,845
And the image of the world's safest-seeming, most glamorous aircraft trailing fire into the ground went around the world in hours.

151
00:12:07,966 --> 00:12:10,160
Chapter five. The verdict.

152
00:12:11,299 --> 00:12:16,003
The investigation fell to the BEA, France's official air accident bureau.

153
00:12:16,003 --> 00:12:22,418
Their job was to reconstruct those ninety seconds, second by second, and answer one question.

154
00:12:22,418 --> 00:12:22,845
Why?

155
00:12:23,966 --> 00:12:29,739
The investigating body, the BEA, published an interim report within months, and its full findings soon after.

156
00:12:29,739 --> 00:12:32,795
Its conclusion was precise, and devastating in its simplicity.

157
00:12:33,933 --> 00:12:38,854
The chain began, the report found, with the metal strip from the Continental DC ten.

158
00:12:38,854 --> 00:12:43,118
It burst the tyre. The tyre debris ruptured the tank. The fuel ignited.

159
00:12:43,118 --> 00:12:48,039
And a routine take off became a catastrophe from which the crew could not recover.

160
00:12:49,166 --> 00:12:52,418
It was, in the language of engineers, a freak accident.

161
00:12:52,418 --> 00:13:02,175
A one in millions sequence, where a small piece of debris found the one vulnerability in an otherwise superb machine, at the one moment it could do the most harm.

162
00:13:03,299 --> 00:13:07,470
The report also noted things that may have made a bad day worse.

163
00:13:07,470 --> 00:13:15,810
The aircraft was reportedly a little over its maximum weight, with a tailwind, and questions were later raised about a missing spacer in the landing gear.

164
00:13:15,810 --> 00:13:19,338
These remain debated. The core chain, the BEA was clear on.

165
00:13:20,466 --> 00:13:25,775
The finding pointed at the Continental strip, and years later, a French court agreed.

166
00:13:25,775 --> 00:13:32,979
In two thousand and ten, it convicted Continental Airlines and one of its mechanics, John Taylor, of involuntary manslaughter.

167
00:13:34,099 --> 00:13:36,313
But that was not the end of it.

168
00:13:36,313 --> 00:13:39,633
In two thousand and twelve, an appeals court overturned the criminal conviction.

169
00:13:39,633 --> 00:13:47,656
It found that Taylor could not possibly have foreseen that so small a part could cause such a disaster, and that his mistake did not amount to a crime.

170
00:13:48,799 --> 00:13:54,957
Continental was still held partly responsible in civil terms, and made to pay a share of the compensation.

171
00:13:54,957 --> 00:13:59,746
But no one, in the end, was found criminally guilty of causing the crash.

172
00:13:59,746 --> 00:14:03,166
It really was, as the engineers had said, an accident.

173
00:14:04,299 --> 00:14:07,785
And here is the cruel irony at the heart of this story.

174
00:14:07,785 --> 00:14:09,818
The crash was not really Concorde's fault.

175
00:14:09,818 --> 00:14:13,013
The machine had flown flawlessly for a quarter of a century.

176
00:14:13,013 --> 00:14:16,498
But the world would never quite see it the same way again.

177
00:14:17,633 --> 00:14:20,402
Chapter six. The comeback that couldn't.

178
00:14:21,533 --> 00:14:26,002
Weeks after the crash, regulators withdrew Concorde's certificate of airworthiness.

179
00:14:26,002 --> 00:14:32,258
For the first time in twenty four years, every Concorde on Earth was grounded.

180
00:14:32,258 --> 00:14:34,046
Not retired. Just, silent.

181
00:14:35,166 --> 00:14:38,701
But British Airways and Air France were not ready to let it die.

182
00:14:38,701 --> 00:14:45,772
They poured money and engineering into a fix, to make sure the one weakness that killed flight four five nine oh could never do it again.

183
00:14:46,899 --> 00:14:55,643
The fuel tanks were lined with flexible Kevlar, the material used in bulletproof vests, so a burst tyre could never rupture them again.

184
00:14:55,643 --> 00:14:59,065
Michelin built special burst-resistant tyres. The wiring was armoured.

185
00:14:59,065 --> 00:15:01,345
It cost around seventeen million pounds.

186
00:15:02,466 --> 00:15:03,549
And it worked.

187
00:15:03,549 --> 00:15:10,770
On the seventh of November, two thousand and one, Concorde flew paying passengers again, across the Atlantic to New York.

188
00:15:10,770 --> 00:15:12,575
The supersonic age was back.

189
00:15:13,699 --> 00:15:16,988
But look at that date again. November two thousand and one.

190
00:15:16,988 --> 00:15:20,276
Less than two months earlier, the eleventh of September had happened.

191
00:15:20,276 --> 00:15:24,461
And the world Concorde was returning to was not the world it had left.

192
00:15:25,599 --> 00:15:27,457
Concorde lived on one thing.

193
00:15:27,457 --> 00:15:34,885
The willingness of the world's richest, busiest people to pay a fortune to save a few hours crossing the Atlantic.

194
00:15:34,885 --> 00:15:38,600
After September eleventh, that trade collapsed. Premium travellers stayed home.

195
00:15:38,600 --> 00:15:40,828
The famous cabin flew half empty.

196
00:15:41,966 --> 00:15:43,925
Chapter seven. The decision.

197
00:15:45,066 --> 00:15:50,662
There was a deeper problem too, one that had been growing for years. Concorde was old.

198
00:15:50,662 --> 00:15:57,657
The fleet was built in the nineteen seventies, and keeping such a rare, extreme machine flying was becoming ruinously expensive.

199
00:15:58,799 --> 00:16:02,995
And then came the blow that no amount of money could fix.

200
00:16:02,995 --> 00:16:07,889
Airbus, the company that had inherited responsibility for supporting Concorde, announced it would stop.

201
00:16:07,889 --> 00:16:10,685
No more spare parts. No more official support.

202
00:16:11,833 --> 00:16:18,976
Without the manufacturer standing behind it, no airline could legally, or safely, keep Concorde in the air for long.

203
00:16:18,976 --> 00:16:22,360
The decision, in truth, had been made for them.

204
00:16:23,499 --> 00:16:30,190
On the tenth of April, two thousand and three, British Airways and Air France announced it together.

205
00:16:30,190 --> 00:16:33,339
After twenty seven years, Concorde would be retired.

206
00:16:33,339 --> 00:16:38,455
The chief executive of British Airways, Rod Eddington, called it a commercial decision.

207
00:16:38,455 --> 00:16:41,210
Falling revenue, rising costs. A sad day.

208
00:16:42,333 --> 00:16:43,643
Air France went first.

209
00:16:43,643 --> 00:16:48,885
Its final commercial Concorde flight landed on the thirty first of May, two thousand and three.

210
00:16:48,885 --> 00:16:54,454
In June, one last Air France Concorde flew home to Toulouse, where the whole story had begun.

211
00:16:55,599 --> 00:16:57,619
British Airways held on until autumn.

212
00:16:57,619 --> 00:17:04,350
And on the twenty fourth of October, two thousand and three, it staged a goodbye the world would never forget.

213
00:17:05,499 --> 00:17:09,230
Three Concordes came home to London Heathrow within minutes of each other.

214
00:17:09,230 --> 00:17:17,933
One from Edinburgh, one from a final supersonic loop over the Bay of Biscay, and the very last one carrying passengers, home from New York, one final time.

215
00:17:19,066 --> 00:17:23,412
Tens of thousands of people lined the fences to watch. Grown men and women wept.

216
00:17:23,412 --> 00:17:26,309
They knew they were not just watching a plane land.

217
00:17:26,309 --> 00:17:28,627
They were watching the future being switched off.

218
00:17:29,766 --> 00:17:30,753
One flight remained.

219
00:17:30,753 --> 00:17:42,594
On the twenty sixth of November, two thousand and three, the last Concorde ever built made the last Concorde flight there would ever be, from Heathrow to Filton, the field where its British half was born.

220
00:17:42,594 --> 00:17:45,883
Its wheels touched down, and the supersonic age was over.

221
00:17:47,033 --> 00:17:49,306
Chapter eight. The silence since.

222
00:17:50,433 --> 00:17:52,941
It has now been more than twenty years.

223
00:17:52,941 --> 00:17:58,583
And in all that time, not a single passenger has flown faster than sound on a scheduled airline.

224
00:17:58,583 --> 00:18:03,599
We went to the Moon, and came back. We built supersonic travel, and gave it up.

225
00:18:04,733 --> 00:18:08,694
Why has nothing replaced it? The same reasons that always haunted Concorde.

226
00:18:08,694 --> 00:18:13,315
It burned far too much fuel. It could only go supersonic over the sea.

227
00:18:13,315 --> 00:18:19,257
And the market of people willing to pay for pure speed was always, in the end, too small.

228
00:18:20,399 --> 00:18:25,953
Today the survivors sit still, in museums around the world. New York and Seattle.

229
00:18:25,953 --> 00:18:29,919
Bristol and Manchester. Paris and Barbados. Beautiful, silent, going nowhere.

230
00:18:29,919 --> 00:18:35,472
Children press their hands against the glass and cannot believe it was ever real.

231
00:18:36,599 --> 00:18:37,968
There are new attempts.

232
00:18:37,968 --> 00:18:48,230
A company called Boom is building a supersonic airliner, and in twenty twenty five its small test jet became the first civilian aircraft to break the sound barrier since Concorde.

233
00:18:48,230 --> 00:18:50,966
Maybe the dream is not dead. Only sleeping.

234
00:18:52,099 --> 00:18:56,132
What stays with me about Concorde is not the crash. Crashes happen.

235
00:18:56,132 --> 00:18:59,492
What stays with me is the choice we made afterward.

236
00:19:00,633 --> 00:19:05,797
For twenty seven years, ordinary people could look up and see the future flying overhead.

237
00:19:05,797 --> 00:19:10,272
Twice the speed of sound. On the edge of space. Real, and routine.

238
00:19:11,399 --> 00:19:17,350
And then, quietly, we decided it cost too much. We had the technology to fly like that.

239
00:19:17,350 --> 00:19:20,150
We still do. We simply chose to stop.

240
00:19:21,299 --> 00:19:25,124
Concorde is the only time in history that human travel got slower.

241
00:19:25,124 --> 00:19:32,453
A reminder that progress is not a straight line, and that some futures, once switched off, are very hard to switch back on.

242
00:19:33,599 --> 00:19:38,124
If the story of Concorde moved you, there is another mindwired film you should see next.

243
00:19:38,124 --> 00:19:41,801
The story of twenty one astronauts who left Earth, and never came home.

244
00:19:41,801 --> 00:19:46,608
It is on screen now. And if you want more stories like this one, subscribe to mindwired.

245
00:19:47,733 --> 00:19:49,535
Subscribe to mindwired.

